    "blocks": [
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "The Asciidoctor CLI can convert multiple files in a single invocation. If you pass multiple source filenames or a filename pattern to the CLI, Asciidoctor will convert each file in turn."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Let’s assume there exist two AsciiDoc files in the current directory, "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "a.adoc"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "emphasis",
            "roles": [
              "path"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "b.adoc"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "emphasis",
            "roles": [
              "path"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ". You can pass both files to Asciidoctor using a single command, as follows:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor a.adoc b.adoc"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Asciidoctor will convert both files, transforming "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "a.adoc"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "emphasis",
            "roles": [
              "path"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "a.html"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "emphasis",
            "roles": [
              "path"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "b.adoc"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "emphasis",
            "roles": [
              "path"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " to "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "b.html"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "emphasis",
            "roles": [
              "path"
            ]
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "To save some typing, you can use the glob operator ("
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "*"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") to match all AsciiDoc files in the current directory using a single argument:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor *.adoc"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Your shell will automatically expand the pattern and interpret the command exactly as you had typed it above:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor a.adoc b.adoc"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "You can pass all AsciiDoc files inside direct subfolders using the glob operator ("
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "*"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ") in place of the directory name:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor */*.adoc"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "To match all files in the current directory and direct subfolders, combine both glob patterns:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor *.adoc */*.adoc"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Since the globs in this command rely on shell expansion, the command is not portable across platforms. To make it portable, you can allow the Asciidoctor CLI to expand the globs. To do so, instruct the shell to not expand the glob by quoting the pattern, as shown here:"
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor '*.adoc' '*/*.adoc'"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "This time, the arguments "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "*.adoc"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " and "
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "*/*.adoc"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": " are passed directly to Asciidoctor instead of being expanded. Asciidoctor handles the glob matching in a manner that is portable across platforms."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "But it gets better. The glob handling in Asciidoctor (which matches the rules of file globbing in Ruby) is likely more powerful than what your shell offers. For example, you can match AsciiDoc files in the current folder and in folders of any depth using the double glob operator ("
          },
          {
            "id": null,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "value": "**"
              }
            ],
            "kind": "monospace",
            "roles": []
          },
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": ")."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "literal",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "$ asciidoctor '**/*.adoc'"
          }
        ],
        "language": null,
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "Most shells do not honor this double glob pattern."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      },
      {
        "context": "paragraph",
        "id": null,
        "inlines": [
          {
            "kind": "text",
            "value": "In conclusion, when specifying a glob pattern, we always recommend enclosing the argument in quotes."
          }
        ],
        "roles": [],
        "title": null
      }
    ],
    "outline": null,
    "title": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "value": "Process Multiple Source Files"
      }
    ]
  }
}
